IMPORT prelude

-- Corruption, Drug Trafficking and Other Serious Crimes (Confiscation of Benefits) Act 1992 (Singapore) -- the parts an ordinary
-- person or business meets: the duty to report a suspicion met at work (s 45, with s 46), the money-laundering offences for benefits
-- from criminal conduct (ss 51, 54, with the scam caning in ss 51(8), (9) and 54(8)), possessing suspicious property (s 55), the
-- "money mule" offences (s 55A), tipping-off (s 57), reporting cash carried across the border or received from abroad (ss 60, 62,
-- 64), and the composition caps (s 81).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition ("incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021"), informal consolidation marked
-- "version in force from 17/8/2026", as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/CDTOSCCBA1992.txt. The latest amendment
-- annotated in the text is S 556/2026 wef 17/08/2026 (to the Second Schedule); the latest amending Act annotated is Act 21 of 2025
-- wef 30/12/2025 (the serious scam caning and the Fourth Schedule).
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0052 in ../../../requirements.jsonl: Tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. It asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a sharper
-- question yet. SCOPED to the offences and reporting duties above. Not encoded: confiscation orders and the assessment of benefits
-- (Parts 2 to 4A), restraint and charging orders, production orders and search powers (Part 5 Divisions 1, 2 and 4), record-keeping
-- by financial institutions (ss 43, 44), the drug-dealing twins of the offences (ss 50, 53 -- same structure and penalties as
-- ss 51, 54 apart from the scam caning, which they lack), cash transaction reports by prescribed persons (Part 6B), the search powers
-- in s 63 (1) to (9), and the four Schedules of predicate offences beyond the one-line Fourth Schedule.
--
-- THE PRESCRIBED AMOUNT for ss 60, 62 and 64 is NOT in the Act; it is set by regulations, which were not deposited. It is a
-- parameter here, and the test file supplies an assumed figure.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Confiscation of Benefits Act -- laundering and reporting`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the higher of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT LEAST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

§§ `45 and 46 -- the duty to report a suspicion met at work`

-- s 45(1): where a person "knows or has reasonable grounds to suspect" that property represents the proceeds of, was used in, or is
-- intended for use in drug dealing or criminal conduct, AND the information came to the person's attention "in the course of the
-- person's trade, profession, business or employment", the person must disclose it to a Suspicious Transaction Reporting Officer
-- "as soon as is reasonably practicable". (2): regardless of whether the transaction was completed.
-- (3): individual $250,000 or 3 years or both; not an individual $500,000.
-- (5): no offence for an advocate and solicitor (or a legal counsel, about the employer) failing to disclose an item subject to
-- legal privilege, or for an arbitrator failing to disclose what came to the arbitrator's attention in the arbitral proceedings.
-- (6): defence of reasonable excuse. (8): defence for an employee who disclosed "in accordance with the procedure established by
-- the person's employer". (7): a good-faith disclosure is not a breach of any restriction imposed by law, contract or rules of
-- professional conduct, and the discloser is not liable for any loss arising out of it.
DECLARE Holder IS ONE OF
    `an ordinary person or business`
    `an advocate and solicitor holding a privileged item`
    `a legal counsel holding a privileged item about the employer`
    `an arbitrator who learnt it in the arbitration`

GIVEN `knows or suspects` IS A BOOLEAN, `learnt it at work` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `must report the suspicion` IF
    `knows or suspects` AND `learnt it at work`

GIVEN `the holder` IS A Holder, `knows or suspects` IS A BOOLEAN, `learnt it at work` IS A BOOLEAN, `reported to an STRO` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN, `reported under the employer's procedure` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `failing to report is an offence` IF
        `must report the suspicion` `knows or suspects` `learnt it at work`
    AND (NOT `reported to an STRO`)
    AND (NOT `had a reasonable excuse`)
    AND (NOT `reported under the employer's procedure`)
    AND `the holder` EQUALS `an ordinary person or business`

GIVEN `is an individual` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine for failing to report` MEANS
    IF `is an individual` THEN 250000 ELSE 500000

§§ `51 and 54 -- laundering benefits from criminal conduct`

-- s 54(1): a person who conceals, disguises, converts, transfers, removes, acquires, possesses or uses property representing "the
-- person's benefits from criminal conduct" commits an offence -- no mental element is written for one's own benefits.
-- s 54(2), (3): the same acts with ANOTHER person's benefits, "knowing or having reasonable grounds to believe".
-- s 54(3A) (from 8 February 2024): the same acts done "rashly" or "negligently".
-- s 51(1): entering into or being concerned in an arrangement facilitating another's retention or control of benefits, knowing or
-- having reasonable grounds to believe; s 51(1A): the same done rashly or negligently.
-- s 51(3)(a): no offence if the person disclosed to an authorised officer (i) BEFORE the act, the act being done "with the consent
-- of the authorised officer", or (ii) AFTER the act, "on the person's initiative and as soon as it is reasonable". s 51(4)(c):
-- defence of an intended disclosure with a reasonable excuse for not making it; (4)(d): employee disclosure under the employer's
-- procedure. s 51(4)(a), (b): defence of not knowing and having no reasonable ground to believe (the "no fault" state below).
-- s 56(2): it is enough that the person knew or had reason to believe the property was the benefits "of an offence generally".
DECLARE Fault IS ONE OF
    `knowing or with reasonable grounds to believe`
    `rashly`
    `negligently`
    `without knowledge or reasonable grounds`

DECLARE Disclosure IS ONE OF
    `no disclosure`
    `disclosed before acting and acted with the officer's consent`
    `disclosed before acting but acted without consent`
    `disclosed after acting on own initiative as soon as reasonable`
    `disclosed after acting only when questioned`
    `intended to disclose and had a reasonable excuse for not doing so`
    `disclosed at work under the employer's procedure`

GIVEN `the disclosure` IS A Disclosure
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the disclosure protects under section 51` IF
    CONSIDER `the disclosure`
    WHEN `disclosed before acting and acted with the officer's consent` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `disclosed after acting on own initiative as soon as reasonable` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `intended to disclose and had a reasonable excuse for not doing so` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `disclosed at work under the employer's procedure` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the fault` IS A Fault, `the disclosure` IS A Disclosure
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the arrangement is an offence under section 51` IF
        (NOT (`the fault` EQUALS `without knowledge or reasonable grounds`))
    AND (NOT (`the disclosure protects under section 51` `the disclosure`))

GIVEN `own benefits` IS A BOOLEAN, `the fault` IS A Fault
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `dealing with the property is an offence under section 54` IF
        `own benefits`
    OR  (NOT (`the fault` EQUALS `without knowledge or reasonable grounds`))

-- ss 51(5)-(7), 54(5)-(7): an individual, knowing limb, $500,000 or 10 years or both; rashly, $250,000 or 5 years; negligently,
-- $150,000 or 3 years. A person who is not an individual: "$1 million or twice the value of the benefits ... whichever is higher".
-- Self-laundering under s 54(1) carries the knowing-limb penalty (s 54(5)).
GIVEN `is an individual` IS A BOOLEAN, `the fault` IS A Fault, `benefits value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum laundering fine` MEANS
    IF NOT `is an individual` THEN `the higher of` 1000000 (2 TIMES `benefits value`)
    ELSE IF `the fault` EQUALS `knowing or with reasonable grounds to believe` THEN 500000
         ELSE IF `the fault` EQUALS `rashly` THEN 250000
              ELSE IF `the fault` EQUALS `negligently` THEN 150000
                   ELSE 0

GIVEN `the fault` IS A Fault
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum laundering prison years for an individual` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the fault`
    WHEN `knowing or with reasonable grounds to believe` THEN 10
    WHEN `rashly` THEN 5
    WHEN `negligently` THEN 3
    OTHERWISE 0

-- ss 51(8), (9), 54(8) (from 30 December 2025): an individual convicted under s 51(1) or s 54(1), (2) or (3) is ALSO liable to
-- caning of not more than 12 strokes if the property is a person's benefits from a "serious scam offence" (Fourth Schedule: an
-- offence under s 420(2) of the Penal Code 1871) and -- for another person's benefits -- the individual "is not able to prove ...
-- that he or she had taken reasonable steps". For one's own benefits under s 54(1) the reasonable-steps escape is not offered.
-- The rash and negligent limbs (ss 51(1A), 54(3A)) carry no caning. s 54(9): no need to prove anyone was convicted of the scam.
GIVEN `is an individual` IS A BOOLEAN, `own benefits` IS A BOOLEAN, `the fault` IS A Fault, `benefits of a serious scam` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proved reasonable steps` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `liable to caning for laundering scam benefits` IF
        `is an individual`
    AND `benefits of a serious scam`
    AND (   `own benefits`
         OR (    `the fault` EQUALS `knowing or with reasonable grounds to believe`
             AND (NOT `proved reasonable steps`)))

§§ `55 -- possessing property reasonably suspected to be benefits`

-- s 55(1): possessing or using property "that may be reasonably suspected" of representing benefits of drug dealing or criminal
-- conduct is an offence "if the person fails to account satisfactorily how the person came by the property". (2): individual
-- $150,000 or 3 years or both; not an individual $300,000.
GIVEN `possesses or uses the property` IS A BOOLEAN, `reasonably suspected` IS A BOOLEAN, `accounted satisfactorily` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `possession is an offence under section 55` IF
    `possesses or uses the property` AND `reasonably suspected` AND (NOT `accounted satisfactorily`)

GIVEN `is an individual` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine under section 55` MEANS
    IF `is an individual` THEN 150000 ELSE 300000

§§ `55A -- lending an account or moving money without asking`

-- s 55A(1), (2) (from 8 February 2024): being concerned in an arrangement, or dealing with property, involving another's benefits
-- is an offence in any of these circumstances: (i) the value "is disproportionate to A's known sources of income"; (ii) A enables
-- another to "access, operate or control a payment account" A controls and "fails to take reasonable steps to ascertain the
-- purpose"; (iii) money is received in or transferred from A's payment account and A "fails to take reasonable steps to ascertain
-- the source or destination of the money"; (iv) A receives money from or transfers it to another and "fails to take reasonable
-- steps to ascertain" that person's "identity and physical location".
-- (3), (4): defence "that the person did not know and had no reasonable ground to believe" that the property was benefits.
-- (5): individual $50,000 or 3 years or both; (6) not an individual $1 million or twice the value, whichever is higher.
-- (In s 55A(2) limb (ii) is confined to the acquiring, possessing or using case; that refinement is not modelled.)
DECLARE Circumstance IS ONE OF
    `none of the listed circumstances`
    `value disproportionate to known income`
    `let another person operate my payment account`
    `money moved through my payment account`
    `money paid to or received from another person`

GIVEN `the circumstance` IS A Circumstance, `took reasonable steps to check` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 55A circumstance is present` IF
    CONSIDER `the circumstance`
    WHEN `none of the listed circumstances` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `value disproportionate to known income` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE NOT `took reasonable steps to check`

GIVEN `the circumstance` IS A Circumstance, `took reasonable steps to check` IS A BOOLEAN, `proved no knowledge or reasonable ground` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the conduct is an offence under section 55A` IF
        `a section 55A circumstance is present` `the circumstance` `took reasonable steps to check`
    AND (NOT `proved no knowledge or reasonable ground`)

GIVEN `is an individual` IS A BOOLEAN, `benefits value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine under section 55A` MEANS
    IF `is an individual` THEN 50000 ELSE `the higher of` 1000000 (2 TIMES `benefits value`)

§§ `57 -- tipping-off`

-- s 57(1), (2): a person who knows or has reasonable grounds to suspect that an authorised officer is investigating (or proposing
-- to), or that a disclosure has been made to an authorised officer, and "discloses to any other person information ... which is
-- likely to prejudice" the investigation, commits an offence: $250,000 or 3 years or both. No separate figure for a person who is
-- not an individual is written.
-- (3), (4): an advocate and solicitor (or legal counsel) may disclose to the client (or employer) in giving advice, or for legal
-- proceedings; (5) not "with a view to furthering any illegal purpose". (6): defence that the person did not know and had no
-- reasonable ground to suspect the disclosure was likely to be prejudicial. (7): enforcement officers acting as such are excepted.
GIVEN `knows or suspects an investigation or report` IS A BOOLEAN, `told someone something likely to prejudice it` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a lawyer advising the client or for proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN, `to further an illegal purpose` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proved no reason to think it prejudicial` IS A BOOLEAN, `acting in enforcement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `tipping-off is an offence` IF
        `knows or suspects an investigation or report`
    AND `told someone something likely to prejudice it`
    AND (NOT (`a lawyer advising the client or for proceedings` AND (NOT `to further an illegal purpose`)))
    AND (NOT `proved no reason to think it prejudicial`)
    AND (NOT `acting in enforcement`)

GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine for tipping-off` MEANS 250000

§§ `60, 62 and 64 -- cash across the border`

-- s 60(1): a person must not move cash into or out of Singapore "the total value of which exceeds the prescribed amount (or its
-- equivalent in a foreign currency)" without giving a report. "Cash" is physical currency or a bearer negotiable instrument
-- (s 59(1)); foreign currency is translated at the exchange rate applicable at the time (s 59(2)). (2): $50,000 or 3 years or
-- both. (3): defence that the person did not know and had no reasonable ground to believe the receptacle contained cash.
-- (6): not a commercial passenger carrier for cash in its passengers' possession; (7): not a commercial goods carrier carrying
-- for another who did not disclose the cash, where the carrier did not know and had no reasonable ground to believe it.
DECLARE Mover IS ONE OF
    `a traveller or sender`
    `a passenger carrier for its passengers' cash`
    `a goods carrier unaware of undisclosed cash`

GIVEN `the mover` IS A Mover, `cash value in dollars` IS A NUMBER, `the prescribed amount` IS A NUMBER, `reported the movement` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proved no reason to know of the cash` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `moving the cash is an offence under section 60` IF
        `the mover` EQUALS `a traveller or sender`
    AND `cash value in dollars` GREATER THAN `the prescribed amount`
    AND (NOT `reported the movement`)
    AND (NOT `proved no reason to know of the cash`)

-- s 62(1): a person who receives cash exceeding the prescribed amount "which is moved to the person from outside Singapore" must
-- report it "before the end of the period of 5 business days beginning on the day of the receipt". "Business day" excludes
-- Saturdays, Sundays and public holidays (s 59(1)). (2): $50,000 or 3 years or both. (3): defence of no reason to believe the
-- cash came from outside Singapore. Below, the day of receipt (if a business day) is business day 1.
GIVEN `business day of the report` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the receipt report is on time` IF
    `business day of the report` AT MOST 5

GIVEN `cash value in dollars` IS A NUMBER, `the prescribed amount` IS A NUMBER, `reported` IS A BOOLEAN, `business day of the report` IS A NUMBER,
      `proved no reason to know it came from abroad` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `receiving the cash is an offence under section 62` IF
        `cash value in dollars` GREATER THAN `the prescribed amount`
    AND (    (NOT `reported`)
         OR  (NOT (`the receipt report is on time` `business day of the report`)))
    AND (NOT `proved no reason to know it came from abroad`)

-- s 64: on conviction under s 60(2) or 62(2) the court may make a confiscation order "in respect of any part of the cash ... that
-- exceeds the prescribed amount".
GIVEN `cash value in dollars` IS A NUMBER, `the prescribed amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most cash that may be confiscated` MEANS
    IF `cash value in dollars` GREATER THAN `the prescribed amount` THEN `cash value in dollars` MINUS `the prescribed amount` ELSE 0

§§ `81 -- composition`

-- s 81(1): an offence "prescribed as a compoundable offence" may be compounded for a sum not exceeding (a) $20,000 for an offence
-- under s 60(2) or 62(2); (b) $5,000 for any other offence. Which offences are prescribed is in regulations, not deposited.
DECLARE CompoundableOffence IS ONE OF
    `failing to report a border cash movement`
    `failing to report cash received from abroad`
    `another prescribed offence`

GIVEN `the offence` IS A CompoundableOffence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the composition cap` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `another prescribed offence` THEN 5000
    OTHERWISE 20000
